   "## A boy without a mother",
   "Rhys was born in 1132 into a family fighting for its life. His grandfather, King Rhys ap Tewdwr, had been killed by the Normans in 1093, and the kingdom of Deheubarth had been carved up between Norman lords. His father, Gruffudd ap Rhys, had come back from exile in Ireland to fight for it from the forests of Cantref Mawr. His aunt was Nest, the most famous woman in Wales.",
   "In 1136, when Rhys was four years old, his mother Gwenllian led an army against the Norman castle at Kidwelly while his father was away seeking help in Gwynedd. She fell in the battle, by the field still called Maes Gwenllian. Later that year her brothers, Owain and Cadwaladr of Gwynedd, joined Rhys’s father and won the great victory of Crug Mawr near Cardigan. A year later his father was dead too. Rhys grew up with his elder brothers, in a land still held by the enemy.",
   "## The brothers win back the south",
   "The sons of Gruffudd ap Rhys did not wait to be men before they fought. In the 1140s and early 1150s Cadell, Maredudd and Rhys took back castle after castle: Carmarthen, Llansteffan, Wiston, Tenby, and the lands of Ceredigion. Rhys was in the fighting from the age of about fourteen. Cadell was wounded and went on pilgrimage; Maredudd died young in 1155. At twenty-three, Rhys ap Gruffudd was ruler of Deheubarth.",
   "## Standing against Henry II",
   "His enemy was the most powerful king in Europe. Henry II ruled England and half of France, and in 1158 he marched into Wales and forced Rhys to give up most of his lands. Rhys bided his time. In 1164 he rose again, and the next year he joined Owain Gwynedd, Owain Cyfeiliog of Powys and the other princes of Wales in the greatest Welsh alliance of the age.",
   "In the summer of 1165 Henry came against them with a huge army from England, Normandy, Anjou and Flanders. The princes of Wales gathered at Corwen and waited. Henry’s army struggled up into the Berwyn mountains, and the rain came down. The army was trapped in the storms and the mud, short of food, and Henry had to turn back to England without a battle. Rhys came home and took Cardigan Castle and Cilgerran, and among his prisoners was Robert FitzStephen, Nest’s son, his own cousin, whom he later let go to win his fortune in Ireland.",
   "## The Justice of South Wales",
   "In 1171 Henry II came west again, this time as a friend. He needed peace in Wales while he went to Ireland, and he knew that Rhys was the man who held the south. The king and the prince made a lasting peace. Henry confirmed Rhys in all the lands he had won, and in 1172 named him Justice of South Wales, the king’s own officer over all the south.",
   "For the next seventeen years there was peace in Deheubarth, and Rhys was its master. The Welsh called him Yr Arglwydd Rhys, the Lord Rhys, a title no other prince of his day held, and the chronicles used it as his name. He ruled from Dinefwr on its crag above the Tywi, and he rebuilt Cardigan Castle in stone and mortar, one of the first stone castles raised by a Welsh prince.",
   "## Christmas at Cardigan, 1176",
   "The chronicle of the princes tells the story of the feast of 1176. The Lord Rhys held a special feast at Cardigan Castle at Christmas, and had it announced a year ahead throughout Wales and England, Scotland and Ireland and the other islands. He set two kinds of contest: one between the bards and poets, and one between the harpers, the crwth-players, the pipers and every kind of musician. And he set two chairs for the winners, and loaded them with great gifts.",
   "A young man of Rhys’s own court, the son of Eilon the crwth-player, won the chair for music. The men of Gwynedd won the chair for poetry. Rhys gave every one of the contestants gifts, and no one went away empty-handed. Every eisteddfod since, from the National Eisteddfod to the smallest chapel eisteddfod in the country, follows the pattern that Rhys set at Cardigan: contests in poetry and music, before the whole nation, with a chair for the winner. In 1976 the National Eisteddfod came to Cardigan to mark eight hundred years.",
   "## Prince of the Church and of learning",
   "Rhys gave to the Church as generously as he gave to the poets. He took the Cistercian abbey of Strata Florida under his care in 1165 and gave it the lands that made it the greatest abbey in Wales, where the monks would later keep the chronicle of the princes and where the princes of Deheubarth were buried. He founded the abbey of Talley and the nunnery of Llanllŷr, and he was a patron of Whitland. The monks of Wales prayed for him as their founder.",
   "His court welcomed poets and learned men, and his own family carried the learning of Wales. When Archbishop Baldwin of Canterbury came to Wales in 1188 to preach the Crusade, Rhys met him at Radnor and rode with him through his lands, and with them went Gerald of Wales, Nest’s grandson, Rhys’s own kinsman, who wrote it all down. Gerald described Rhys as a man of noble bearing and generous heart, and the Journey through Wales is full of the prince’s country.",
   "## The last years",
   "When Henry II died in 1189, the peace died with him. Rhys, now in his late fifties, took up the sword again and swept through the Norman lordships of the south, taking Kidwelly, Laugharne, St Clears and Llansteffan. He was still fighting in his sixties, and in 1196 he won his last campaign, burning Carmarthen and taking the castles of Radnor and Painscastle.",
   "He died on 28 April 1197 and was buried in St Davids Cathedral, where a stone effigy of a knight is still shown as his. The chronicle of the princes mourned him as the head and the shield and the strength of the south and of all Wales, the hope and the defence of all the Britons. It said that he was unconquered in mind and generous in giving, and that the glory of Wales had gone down with him into the grave.",
   "## Why Prince Rhys matters",
   "Rhys ap Gruffudd was four when his mother fell and five when his father died, and the kingdom of his grandfather had been in Norman hands for forty years. By the time he died he had won it all back and held it for forty years, and the greatest king in Europe had had to treat him as an equal.",
   "But his greatest gift outlasted his castles. At Christmas 1176 he gathered the poets and the musicians of Wales at Cardigan and set a chair for the best of them. Eight and a half centuries later the people of Wales still gather every summer to do the same thing. Every chair that has ever been won at an eisteddfod goes back to Prince Rhys."
